key_passage.text:
The Fourier transform of the cosine wave, $\cos{ \left( 2\pi \left( \frac{u_0\, n}{N} + \frac{v_0\, m}{M} \right) \right) }$, is: $$\begin{aligned}
\sum_{n=0}^{N-1} \sum_{m=0}^{M-1} \, \cos{ \left( 2\pi \left( \frac{u_0 \, n}{N} + \frac{v_0 \, m}{M} \right) \right) }
\exp{ \left( -2\pi j \left( \frac{u\, n}{N} + \frac{v\, m}{M} \right) \right)} = \\ =\frac{1}{2} \left( \delta \left[u-u_0, v-v_0 \right] + \delta \left[u+u_0, v+v_0 \right] \right)
\end{aligned}$$ This can be easily proven using Euler's equation and the orthogonality between complex exponentials.

